Sculpt and rig the **Caldera Repeater**, the cheap workhorse tower of the Holdfast — a
slim brass post rising to a rotating drum of many short iron barrels, like a rotary gun —
as a {{voxel.width}}x{{voxel.height}}x{{voxel.depth}} opaque-voxel model using only the
`voxel-anim` tool. You build it by **painting opaque cells** one operation at a time, and
you **author its idle and fire animations** as F-curves.

This test measures **creativity and craft**, not instruction-following: the brief tells
you *what the Repeater is* and *how it must move*, but the parts you build, how you break
the tower into moving pieces, where you place the joints, and how you shape every form are
**entirely yours to invent**. Work out the pieces a tower whose barrel drum spins needs.

## How sculpting and rigging works

The only way to place a voxel and edit the rig is the `voxel-anim` binary already on your
`PATH`. You build the model by calling it **one operation at a time**, and the ordered
operations you issue — recorded per part to `parts/<part>.actions.json` — together with the
rig you define are your submission.

**You invent the rig.** Define whatever parts you need with `define-part` (giving each a
parent and a pivot), place joints on them with `define-joint`, and author the required
animations with `define-animation`/`add-keyframe`. Every voxel operation targets a **part**
with `--part <name>`, so define a part before you sculpt into it; each part is sculpted
**separately** in its own preview and log. The volume starts **empty**. Voxels are **opaque**
`#rrggbb` colors — there is no transparency. Each call **records only** — it appends the
operation to that part's log and renders nothing; run `voxel-anim render` when you want to
(re)draw the per-part previews (`parts/<part>.png`) and the assembled scene.

Each operation is an ordinary subcommand with flags, naming its part, for example:

```
voxel-anim define-part --name post
voxel-anim fill-box --part post --x0 10 --y0 0 --z0 10 --x1 13 --y1 30 --z1 13 --color "#c8a24a"
```

(The part name above is just an example — name your own parts however you like.) Run
`voxel-anim --help` to list every operation **and** the rig subcommands (defining parts,
setting pivots, defining joints, and defining/keyframing animations), and `voxel-anim
<operation> --help` for one operation's exact flags — that help text is the authoritative
contract. **Run `voxel-anim render`, then read `parts/<part>.png`** to see that part's
progress **and the assembled-scene previews under `scene/` (`scene/iso.png`,
`scene/front.png`, `scene/side.png`, `scene/top.png`)** — the whole Repeater composed from
all your parts, drawn when you run `voxel-anim render`. The scene views are how you confirm
the parts actually fit together: that the post stands under the drum, the barrels cluster
evenly around their axis, and the collar rings the join unbroken. Because each part is
sculpted in the shared volume's coordinates, position each part where it belongs on the
finished Repeater; the scene simply composes them there.

